config FPGA_RAM_AS_HEAP
    bool "Use remaining FPGA RAM as FreeRTOS Heap"
    default n
    depends on IPRO6
    help
      In FPGA environment, the "flash" region is actually RAM.
      This option enables using the remaining FPGA RAM (after code/data)
      as an additional FreeRTOS heap region.

      Memory layout with this option:
        - Code region: 2MB @ 0x12000000 (for program execution)
        - Heap region: remaining space after code/rodata/data

      When enabled:
        - Linker script reserves first 2MB for code
        - Remaining flash region becomes __heap_flash_start/size
        - FreeRTOS heap includes both TCM heap and flash heap regions

      This provides significantly more heap memory for FPGA development.

      Say Y if you need more heap memory on FPGA board.

endif  # FPGA

#
# Build Configuration
#
menu "Build Configuration"

choice
    prompt "IPRO7 flash layout"
    depends on IPRO7
    default IPRO7_FLASH_1M
    help
      Select the flash size used by the IPRO7 linker configuration
      (bsp/board/linker/configs/ipro7_flash.cmake).

      1MB matches the on-chip flash on mainstream IPRO7 boards.

      2MB / 4MB / 8MB are only valid on boards with SF3 external flash
      (efuse flash_cfg=2, typically using GPIO 28-33). Also set
      CONFIG_DISABLE_SDC_PINMUX=y when enabling external flash to avoid
      reinitializing the pins used by the external flash.

      WARNING: selecting a larger size than the board physically carries
      will produce a binary that fits the linker budget but cannot be
      flashed / will fail to boot. Only enable on boards you have
      physically verified to carry the external flash.

config IPRO7_FLASH_1M
    bool "1MB (on-chip, default)"

config IPRO7_FLASH_2M
    bool "2MB (SF3 external flash)"

config IPRO7_FLASH_4M
    bool "4MB (SF3 external flash)"

config IPRO7_FLASH_8M
    bool "8MB (SF3 external flash)"

endchoice

config HAL_PIO_SDH
    bool "PIO-based 4-bit SD host (A/B vs hardware SDH)"
    depends on IPRO7
    default n
    help
      Software SD host built on the PIO state machines, on GPIO22-27
      (CLK=22, CMD=23, D0-D3=24-27). Independent of the hardware SDH
      (GPIO28-33), which is left untouched for A/B comparison.
      Pulls in hal_pio_sdh.c, hal_pio_sdh_crc.c and drv_pio.c.

config HAL_PIO_SDH_DAT_GAP
    int "PIO-SDH read inter-nibble gap (CLK-low cycles, 0-30)"
    depends on HAL_PIO_SDH
    default 0
    range 0 30
    help
      Extra CLK-low cycles inserted between DATA nibbles on the read path,
      trading throughput for signal-integrity margin. The 24 MHz wire ceiling
      is 48/(4+gap) MB/s: gap 0 = 12 MB/s (read ~9.4), gap 5 = 5.3 MB/s
      (read ~4.6). gap 0 is clean with High-Speed mode + rising-latch sampling
      (the default after enumeration) on this bench; raise it (e.g. 5) only if
      the card does not grant HS or the wiring is marginal (CRC/data errors at
      gap 0). Runtime override any time: `pio_sdcard gap <0-30>`.

config HAL_PIO_SDH_PAD_DRIVE
    int "PIO-SDH pad drive strength (0-3)"
    depends on HAL_PIO_SDH
    default 3
    range 0 3
    help
      GPIO pad drive strength for all six PIO-SDH pins (CLK/CMD/D0-D3).
      3 (max) holds the lines firmly against SSO coupling on the jumper
      bench; a lower value doubles as source termination if a hard-driven
      edge rings at the card. Runtime override: `pio_sdcard pad`.

config HAL_PIO_SDH_PULL_NONE
    bool "Disable internal pull-ups on PIO-SDH pads"
    depends on HAL_PIO_SDH
    default n
    help
      Leave CMD/D0-D3 (and CLK) without internal pull-ups. Select this
      when the SD adapter board provides external pull-up resistors, so
      the lines see a single defined pull instead of two in parallel.
      Without external pulls this MUST stay off (CMD/DAT float during
      bus turnaround). Runtime override: `pio_sdcard pad`.

config LINK_FLASH
    bool "Link from Flash (instead of RAM)"
    default n
    help
      Select the linker script configuration:
      
      Flash boot (Y):
        - Uses ipro7_flash.ld linker script
        - Flash: 4MB @ 0x13000000
        - OCRAM: 256KB @ 0x11010000
        - Suitable for large applications (BLE 5.4, complex features)
        - Production deployment configuration
      
      RAM boot (N):
        - Uses ipro7_ram.ld linker script
        - Flash: 128KB @ 0x11010000
        - OCRAM: 128KB @ 0x11030000
        - Faster development cycle (no flash programming)
        - Limited code size - suitable for simple applications only
      
        - Say N for quick prototyping with small code size

      Recommendation:
        - Say Y for BLE 5.4 applications and production builds
        - Say N for quick prototyping with small code size

config LINKER_SCRIPT
    string "Custom linker script filename"
    default ""
    help
      Specify a custom linker script file located in the app directory.
      Leave empty to use the default behavior:
        1. apps/<project>/<project>.ld (if exists)
        2. Generated linker script (from board config)

      Example: Set to "custom_64k.ld" to use apps/<project>/custom_64k.ld

config ENABLE_FRAME_POINTER
    bool "Enable Frame Pointer"
    default y
    help
      Enable compilation with frame pointers (-fno-omit-frame-pointer).
      This allows for accurate stack backtracing during exceptions.

      Impact:
        - Slightly increased code size
        - Reduced general purpose registers by 1 (s0 used as FP)
        - Significantly improved debugging capability

config FLASH_CFG_FROM_BOOTROM
    bool "Use flash config from bootrom (tool-only flash porting)"
    default y
    depends on LINK_FLASH
    help
      Use the flash configuration passed from bootrom via boot2 parameters
      instead of the SDK driver's hardcoded flash chip table.

      When enabled:
        - Adding a new flash chip only requires iprocube tool changes
          (.conf file + flashcfg_list.csv + bootheader config)
        - No SDK driver changes needed (no drv_sf_cfg_ext.c modification)
        - Bootrom identifies the flash and passes config to FW

      When disabled:
        - FW detects flash via JEDEC ID and looks up in drv_sf_cfg_ext.c
        - New flash chips require both tool and SDK driver changes

      Say Y for production builds. Say N only if bootrom flash config
      is not available or for debugging flash detection.

config USE_ROM_DRIVERS
    bool "Use ROM Drivers (smaller code size)"
    default n
    depends on IPRO7 || IPRO6
    help
      Use driver functions from ROM instead of full SDK implementations.
      This reduces code size by calling pre-programmed ROM driver functions.

      When enabled:
        - Uses drv_romapi.c + drv_romapi_patch.c
        - Smaller code size (drivers run from ROM)
        - ROM drivers are fixed at ROM programming time

      When disabled:
        - Uses full SDK driver implementations
        - Larger code size but more flexibility
        - Can modify driver behavior

      Note: Some APIs have changed signatures between ROM and SDK.
      The drv_romapi_patch.c file handles these differences.
      See docs/ROMDRIVER_API_DIFF.md for details.

config SINGLE_PRECISION_FLOAT
    bool "Use single-precision (float) instead of double"
    default n
    help
      Add -fsingle-precision-constant to treat unsuffixed floating-point
      literals as float instead of double. Also converts printf %f
      internals and math library calls to float-only variants.
      Saves ~10KB flash on RISC-V soft-float targets.
      No precision impact for typical embedded use (ADC, sensor, ISP).

config EXCEPTION_DUMP
    bool "Enable exception crash dump over UART"
    default y
    help
      Enable crash dump system that streams full memory state to a PC
      tool over UART when an exception or configASSERT failure occurs.

      When enabled:
        - Exception handler waits for PC tool magic sequence
        - Dumps OCRAM, PSRAM, peripheral registers, and CPU state
        - Uses block transfer with CRC-16 checksums at 2 Mbaud
        - PC tool provides automatic crash analysis with backtrace
        - Code size: ~2.4 KB text, ~1 KB BSS (static TX buffer)

      When disabled:
        - Exception handler prints diagnostics and halts (original behavior)
        - No DUMP_READY banner, no memory dump capability
        - Saves ~2.4 KB flash

      Say Y for development and debugging (default).
      Say N for production builds where code size is critical.

config EXCEPTION_AUTO_REBOOT
    bool "Auto reboot on CPU exception"
    default n
    help
      Automatically reboot the system when a CPU exception occurs.

      When enabled:
        - Exception handler prints debug info then reboots
        - Reset reason is set to HAL_RST_REASON_EXCEPTION
        - If EXCEPTION_DUMP is also enabled, dump runs first, then reboots
        - Useful for production systems that need auto-recovery

      When disabled (default):
        - Exception handler prints debug info and loops forever
        - Useful for development and debugging

      Say N for development, Y for production auto-recovery.

endmenu

#
# Memory Configuration
#
menu "Memory Configuration"

config LINK_FLASH
    bool "Enable XIP-Flash Link"
    default y
    help
      Enable SPI Nor Flash XIP

config USE_PSRAM
    bool "Enable PSRAM"
    default n
    help
      Enable PSRAM support

config ENABLE_NVDS
    bool "Enable NVDS (Non-Volatile Data Storage)"
    default y
    help
      Enable NVDS (Non-Volatile Data Storage) section in linker script.
      When enabled, reserves a RAM mirror for BLE/non-volatile data
      storage. The linker default is 1KB and can be overridden with
      NVDS_SIZE.

      Memory impact:
        - Enabled:  NVDS_SIZE bytes reserved from OCRAM by default
        - Disabled: no RAM mirror reserved

      This option is typically used for storing persistent configuration
      and calibration data in RAM-based storage.

      Say Y if you need persistent data storage.
      Say N to maximize available RAM.

config NVDS_SIZE
    string "NVDS RAM mirror size"
    depends on ENABLE_NVDS
    default "1K"
    help
      Size of the linker-reserved NVDS RAM mirror. The default BLE
      controller tags fit in 1KB. Increase only if an application adds more
      NVDS tags or a larger mirror is explicitly required.

      Accepted values are linker expressions such as 1024, 1K or 4K.

config NVDS_IN_PSRAM
    bool "Place NVDS RAM mirror in PSRAM"
    depends on ENABLE_NVDS && USE_PSRAM
    default n
    help
      Place .nvds in PSRAM instead of OCRAM. The IPRO7 linker generator
      anchors __heap_psram_start after MAX(_bss_psram_end, __nvds_end), so
      the FreeRTOS heap cannot overlap the NVDS mirror.

      Leave disabled by default for existing applications. Enable for
      PSRAM-equipped AI/MM/BLE builds where OCRAM headroom is tight.

config DISABLE_EM_TCM_RET
    bool "Disable em_tcm_ret section (merge TCM code into OCRAM)"
    default n
    depends on AI_SUPPORT && MM_SUPPORT
    help
      When AI and MM support are both enabled, the linker normally
      creates a separate em_tcm_ret section (16KB at 0x1102C000)
      for TCM code to maximize OCRAM space for data.

      Enable this option to merge all TCM code back into OCRAM,
      eliminating the em_tcm_ret section. This is useful when:
        - Your OCRAM has enough space for both data and TCM code
        - You want simpler memory layout

      Memory impact (with AI+MM, EM_SIZE=0):
        - Disabled (default): OCRAM=48KB data, em_tcm_ret=16KB code
        - Enabled: OCRAM=48KB (data + TCM code merged)

      Say N unless you know your OCRAM can fit all TCM code.

endmenu

#
# Hardware Features
#
menu "Hardware Features"

config MM_SUPPORT
    bool "Enable Multimedia Support"
    default n
    depends on IPRO7
    select USE_PSRAM
    help
      Enable multimedia hardware features including camera (ISP),
      JPEG encoder/decoder, and related peripherals.
      Note: This automatically enables PSRAM support.
      Only available on IPRO7 platform.

config LDO_SUPPORT
    bool "Use LDO power supply (instead of DCDC)"
    default n
    depends on IPRO7
    help
      Select the primary power supply for the AON domain.

      When disabled (default, DCDC mode):
        - pu_ldo14_aon = 0 (LDO off)
        - pu_dcdc_aon  = 1 (DCDC on)
        - Lower power consumption, requires external inductor

      When enabled (LDO mode):
        - pu_ldo14_aon = 1 (LDO on)
        - pu_dcdc_aon  = 0 (DCDC off)
        - Simpler BOM, no inductor needed

      Say N for boards with DCDC circuit (default).
      Say Y for boards using LDO-only power supply.

config SUPPORT_PURE_DCDC
    bool "Skip DCDC/LDO re-init on HBN wake (requires DCDC mode)"
    default n
    depends on IPRO7 && !LDO_SUPPORT
    help
      Optimise HBN (deep sleep) wake-up path.

      When disabled (default):
        - board_power_init runs every boot, including HBN wake.
        - Cold-boot flow is always applied.

      When enabled:
        - On cold boot (POR), board_power_init runs and programs the
          DCDC + VDD14 bond option + LDO14 disable + vouts as usual.
        - On warm boot (HBN wake, detected via HBN RAM sysinfo magic),
          board_power_init returns immediately. All AON/HBN power
          registers (HBN_CTL pu_dcdc_aon / pu_ldo14_aon, HBN_MISC
          vdd14_bond_option_enb_aon, DCDC18_TOP_0 voutSel, LDO14_TOP
          vout_sel) survive HBN 1-tick wake-up, so re-init is a no-op.
        - Saves the ~10us of power-init overhead per HBN wake and
          avoids transient glitches from redundant reg writes.

      Requires DCDC mode (mutually exclusive with LDO_SUPPORT).

config PURE_DCDC_AON_WDT_RECOVER
    bool "AON WDT safety net for pure_dcdc wakeup"
    default y
    depends on SUPPORT_PURE_DCDC
    help
      Arm the AON watchdog (HBN_WDT) inside pure_dcdc_save_state with
      mask_bit=6 (~3.9ms timeout) to cover the DCDC switch → HBN-1T
      bounce → wakeup stub recovery path. The stub disables WDT at end
      of recovery before FW main runs.

      When enabled (default):
        - Any hang between DCDC switch and end of stub recovery triggers
          AON WDT chip reset (~3.9ms). HBN domain regs (HBN_MISC[11]
          warm marker, HBN_RSV0=EHBN, HBN_RSV1=stub) survive the WDT
          reset so BROM fastboot dispatches to stub on retry.
        - Validated 10000-iter stress: 0 REAL_HANG over 7946 effective
          iterations; ~2.7% WDT recovery rate (always single-fire).

      When disabled:
        - Saves ~30 bytes flash + ~10us setup time per cold boot.
        - Wakeup hangs (silicon-corner-case during HBN bounce or stub
          recovery) require relay/external POR to recover; chip will
          appear bricked from RST_N perspective.

config NMSIS_DSP
    bool "Enable NMSIS DSP Library"
    default y
    depends on IPRO7
    help
      Enable the NMSIS DSP library for RISC-V DSP operations.
      This provides optimized DSP functions including FFT, filters,
      matrix operations, and statistical functions.

      Code size impact: ~80KB (mostly lookup tables for FFT)

      When enabled:
        - Links libnmsis_dsp library
        - Provides riscv_rfft_fast_f32 and other DSP functions
        - Required for NIST DSP acceleration

      When disabled:
        - Saves ~80KB flash
        - NIST spectral test uses software DFT (slower)
        - Other DSP-dependent features may not work

      Say Y if you need DSP functions or fast FFT.
      Say N to save flash if DSP is not needed.

config AON_WDT_ENABLE
    bool "Enable AON Watchdog Timer in FW"
    default n
    depends on IPRO7
    help
      Enable AON WDT management in FW.
      If disabled (default), FW disables AON WDT in system_init.
      If enabled, FW extends WDT to ~32s timeout with 1s kick task.

config DMA_CHANNEL_ALLOCATOR
    bool "Enable runtime DMA channel allocator"
    default n
    help
      Replace hardcoded DMA0_CHx_INDEX constants with a runtime
      allocator that hands out channels by peripheral request ID
      (DMA_REQUEST_DAC0, DMA_REQUEST_AUD_ADC, ...).

      When enabled:
        - bsp/hal exposes dma_request_channel/release_channel/
          dump_allocation APIs.
        - dma_init() splits into a one-shot dma_controller_init()
          plus per-channel dma_channel_attach() so initialising
          channel N does not disrupt in-flight transfers on
          channels 0..N-1.
        - dma_channel_attach() enables the per-channel DMA clock
          via GLB_Set_DMA_CLK — required for CH4..CH7 which are
          clock-gated at reset.

      When disabled (default):
        - Legacy behaviour: dma_init() does a global
          DMA_Disable/Enable each call. CH4..CH7 remain
          unusable (clock-gated).
        - Raw DMA0_CHx_INDEX constants keep working.

      Per-app opt-in: enable per project .config as each app is
      ported onto the allocator. Mixing modes across apps in one
      build is supported for migration.

endmenu

#
# Debug UART Configuration
#
menu "Debug UART Configuration"

config EARLY_PRINT
    bool "Enable early boot print via UART1 TX FIFO"
    default n
    depends on IPRO7
    help
      Output boot stage markers before platform UART is initialized.
      Bootrom leaves UART1 configured at 115200 baud on GPIO 17.
      This remaps UART1 TX to GPIO 20 (debug UART pin) and writes
      directly to the TX FIFO register — zero driver dependencies.

      Markers printed:
        [EP] _premain_init  — entered pre-main init
        [EP] efuse_init     — about to read efuse
        [EP] psram_init     — about to calibrate PSRAM
        [EP] psram_done     — PSRAM calibration complete
        [EP] main           — entered main()

      Say Y to diagnose boot failures before UART is ready.
      Say N for production builds.

config PLATFORM_UART_BAUDRATE
    int "Platform UART Baudrate"
    default 115200
    help
      Configure the baudrate for platform debug UART.
      
      Common baudrate values:
        - 9600
        - 19200
        - 38400
        - 57600
        - 115200 (default)
        - 230400
        - 460800
        - 921600
      
      Default: 115200 bps

endmenu

